::ENG4_U01_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: In professional communication, conventions are not decorative rules; they protect meaning. Which sentence is complete? {
~A. Because conventions protect meaning.#This is a fragment.
=B. Conventions protect meaning in professional communication.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
~C. When conventions protect meaning.#This is a fragment.
~D. Although conventions protect meaning.#This is a fragment.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: In professional communication, conventions are not decorative rules; they protect meaning. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Conventions are mostly decorative in professional writing.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Professional communication should ignore convention rules.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. Conventions matter in professional writing because they preserve meaning.#Correct. This preserves the main idea.
~D. Meaning is unrelated to punctuation and grammar.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas. Which sentence avoids an unnecessary comma? {
=A. A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas.#Correct. No comma interrupts the subject and verb.
~B. A misplaced comma, can blur the relationship between ideas.#The comma incorrectly separates subject and verb.
~C. A misplaced comma can blur, the relationship between ideas.#The comma interrupts the verb phrase.
~D. A misplaced, comma can blur the relationship between ideas.#The comma incorrectly splits the noun phrase.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. Which option has correct subject-verb agreement? {
~A. Clear conventions helps readers evaluate reasoning.#The plural subject needs help.
=B. Clear conventions help readers evaluate reasoning.#Correct. The plural subject and verb agree.
~C. Clear convention help readers evaluate reasoning.#The singular subject needs helps.
~D. Clear conventions has helped readers evaluate reasoning.#The plural subject needs have.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Strong writers revise conventions because accurate language supports credible thinking. What does credible mean? {
~A. Decorative#This is unrelated.
=B. Trustworthy#Correct. Credible means believable or trustworthy.
~C. Fragmented#This is unrelated.
~D. Misplaced#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: When grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling are controlled, the audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation. Which sentence correctly uses commas in a series? {
=A. Writers revise grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling.#Correct. The commas separate items in the series.
~B. Writers revise grammar punctuation capitalization and spelling.#Commas are missing.
~C. Writers revise, grammar, punctuation capitalization, and spelling.#The first comma is misplaced.
~D. Writers revise grammar, punctuation capitalization and, spelling.#The commas are misplaced.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A sentence fragment can make a recommendation sound unfinished. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Fragments make recommendations more complete.#This reverses the meaning.
=B. A fragment can make advice or a proposal seem incomplete.#Correct. This preserves the idea.
~C. Fragments are always the best choice in professional writing.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Recommendations do not need complete sentences.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Strong writers revise conventions because accurate language supports credible thinking. Which revision fixes the agreement error? Original: Accurate language support credible thinking. {
~A. Accurate language supporting credible thinking.#This is a fragment.
=B. Accurate language supports credible thinking.#Correct. The singular subject language takes supports.
~C. Accurate language support credible thinkings.#This has agreement and word-form errors.
~D. Accurate languages supports credible thinking.#This creates another agreement issue.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. What does evaluate mean? {
~A. Ignore completely#This is the opposite of evaluate.
=B. Judge or assess#Correct. Evaluate means judge or assess.
~C. Decorate#This is unrelated.
~D. Fragment#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: When grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling are controlled, the audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation. Which sentence is punctuated correctly? {
=A. When conventions are controlled, readers can focus on reasoning.#Correct. The introductory clause is followed by a comma.
~B. When conventions are controlled readers, can focus on reasoning.#The comma is misplaced.
~C. When, conventions are controlled readers can focus on reasoning.#The comma interrupts the clause.
~D. When conventions are controlled readers can focus, on reasoning.#The needed comma after the introductory clause is missing.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. Which claim is supported by the passage? {
~A. Conventions prevent readers from evaluating reasoning.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Conventions only matter in creative writing.#This is unsupported.
=C. Clear conventions make reasoning easier for readers to evaluate.#Correct. This matches the passage.
~D. Readers should ignore convention errors.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas. Which sentence shows a comma error? {
~A. A misplaced comma can blur meaning.#This is correct.
=B. A misplaced comma, can blur meaning.#Correct. The comma separates subject and verb.
~C. A comma can affect meaning in professional writing.#This is correct.
~D. Misplaced punctuation can confuse readers.#This is correct.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Strong writers revise conventions because accurate language supports credible thinking. What does revise mean? {
~A. Refuse to change#This is the opposite of revise.
~B. Copy without reading#This is not revision.
=C. Review and improve#Correct. Revise means review and improve.
~D. Make unclear#This is not revision.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: When grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling are controlled, the audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation. Which sentence uses capitalization correctly? {
=A. Strong writers revise grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling.#Correct. Only the first word is capitalized.
~B. Strong Writers revise Grammar, punctuation, Capitalization, and spelling.#Unnecessary words are capitalized.
~C. strong writers revise grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling.#The first word should be capitalized.
~D. Strong writers revise grammar, Punctuation, Capitalization, and Spelling.#The list items do not need capitals here.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: In professional communication, conventions are not decorative rules; they protect meaning. Which paraphrase distorts the source? {
~A. Conventions protect meaning in professional communication.#This is accurate.
~B. Convention rules are not merely decorative.#This is accurate.
=C. Conventions matter only because they make writing look attractive.#Correct. This distorts the passage.
~D. Professional writing uses conventions to preserve meaning.#This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. Which sentence fixes the pronoun agreement problem? Original: A writer should revise their sentence for clarity. {
~A. A writer should revise their sentence for clarity.#The singular antecedent and plural pronoun may be unclear in formal agreement contexts.
=B. Writers should revise their sentences for clarity.#Correct. The plural noun matches the plural pronoun.
~C. A writers should revise their sentences for clarity.#This has noun-number errors.
~D. Writers should revises their sentences for clarity.#This has a verb-form error.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas. What does blur mean in this context? {
~A. Make clearer#This is the opposite.
=B. Make less clear#Correct. Blur means make unclear.
~C. Capitalize#This is unrelated.
~D. Finish#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A sentence fragment can make a recommendation sound unfinished. Which option is a fragment? {
~A. A recommendation can sound unfinished.#This is complete.
=B. Because the recommendation lacks a complete thought.#Correct. This is a dependent clause fragment.
~C. The writer revised the recommendation.#This is complete.
~D. The recommendation is clear.#This is complete.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Strong writers revise conventions because accurate language supports credible thinking. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Writers revise conventions because accurate language makes thinking less credible.#This reverses the meaning.
~B. Strong writers ignore convention errors when the idea is good.#This contradicts the passage.
=C. Strong writers edit conventions because precise language helps reasoning seem trustworthy.#Correct. This preserves the idea.
~D. Conventions are unrelated to credibility.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. Which sentence is correct? {
=A. Clear conventions help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning.#Correct. The grammar and possessive are correct.
~B. Clear conventions helps readers evaluate a writer's reasoning.#The verb does not agree.
~C. Clear conventions help reader's evaluate a writers reasoning.#The apostrophes are incorrect.
~D. Clear convention help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning.#The subject and verb do not agree.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: When grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling are controlled, the audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation. Which choice is the best correction? Original: The audience can focus on the claim evidence and explanation. {
~A. The audience can focus on the claim evidence, and explanation.#One comma is still missing.
=B. The audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. The series is clear.
~C. The audience can focus, on the claim evidence and explanation.#The comma is misplaced.
~D. The audience, can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation.#The first comma is incorrect.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: In professional communication, conventions are not decorative rules; they protect meaning. What does decorative mean here? {
=A. Related to appearance rather than meaning#Correct. Decorative means ornamental or appearance-based.
~B. Grammatically required#This is not the meaning.
~C. Trustworthy#This is credible.
~D. Incomplete#This is unfinished.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: When grammar, punctuation, capitalization, and spelling are controlled, the audience can focus on the claim, evidence, and explanation instead of struggling to decode the sentence. What is the relationship between controlled conventions and audience focus? {
~A. Controlled conventions distract the audience from reasoning.#This reverses the relationship.
=B. Controlled conventions help the audience focus on reasoning.#Correct. The passage says readers can focus on claim, evidence, and explanation.
~C. Controlled conventions remove the need for evidence.#This is unsupported.
~D. Controlled conventions make sentences impossible to understand.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas, and a sentence fragment can make a recommendation sound unfinished. Which sentence correctly joins two related independent clauses? {
~A. Conventions protect meaning, they help readers evaluate reasoning.#This is a comma splice.
=B. Conventions protect meaning; they help readers evaluate reasoning.#Correct. A semicolon can join related independent clauses.
~C. Conventions protect meaning they help readers evaluate reasoning.#This is a run-on.
~D. Because conventions protect meaning; they help readers evaluate reasoning.#This incorrectly uses a dependent clause with a semicolon.
}
::ENG4_U01_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Strong writers revise conventions because accurate language supports credible thinking. Which sentence is most clear and conventional? {
=A. Accurate language supports credible thinking, so strong writers revise convention errors.#Correct. The sentence is clear and conventional.
~B. Accurate language support credible thinking so strong writers revises errors.#This has agreement errors.
~C. Accurate language supports credible thinking strong writers revise errors.#This is a run-on.
~D. Accurate language, supports credible thinking, so strong writers, revise errors.#The commas are misplaced.
}
